{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a requirement, its complete fenced declaration MUST\nbe included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is met. The evidence is mildly indirect on the \"complete fenced declaration\" point, as noted below.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `AssessmentSources::prepare` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` is marked `{impl}` for `requirements.authoring_input`.\n- It builds its selector list starting with `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }`, so the subject's own declaration is always the first mandatory input.\n- `source_input` resolves that selector. For `Requirements` selectors it passes the file access check and never omits them: `generated_evidence_selector` returns false for `Requirements` and `Definition` selectors.\n- The resolved text is the declaration's `raw_lines`, which is the whole fenced block from the `:::{req} …` opener through the closing `:::`. Only option lines such as `:impl:` or `:test:` are left out.\n- The mandatory inputs in this task are an example of this: the subject's text appears as `\":::{req} authoring_input\"` through `\":::\"`.\n- The subject is not limited to requirements. For an example subject, the same selector yields the example's declaration. The test checks this with `mandatory_inputs()[0][\"requirements\"] == \"requirements.case\"`.\n- The subject input is added once, before the cited requirements, anchors, and `{impl}`/`{test}` sources. `insert_selector` and the `mandatory.contains` check deduplicate, as `requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs` requires.\n- The subject input is returned in the initial bundle that `initial_prompt` serializes into the first request.\n- `prepare` does nothing here beyond what the requirement and its sibling contribution requirements specify.\n\n**Tests**\n- `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It prepares both a requirement subject and an example subject, and checks that the example's own declaration is the first input.\n- `mandatory_git_inputs_require_staging_declarations_sources_and_terms` is also marked `{test}`. It shows that the subject's declaration file `md/requirements.md` is a mandatory input whose read is enforced, because `prepare` fails until the file is staged with `git add`.\n- Other tests in the same module check the complete declaration text but do not cite this requirement:\n  - `anchored_files_contribute_root_node_inputs` asserts the exact first input `[\":::{req} ready\", \"The project MUST be ready.\", \":::\"]`.\n  - `unresolvable_earlier_inputs_do_not_block_reassessment` asserts the same for a sole input.\n  - `requirements_inputs_omit_option_lines` in `src/evidence.rs` asserts that the resolved text equals the raw fenced lines.\n- Missing-subject and failure cases are covered: `prepare(..., \"missing\")` is an error, and a disallowed declaration file aborts (`disallowed_mandatory_inputs_abort_preparation`).\n\n**Minor observations (not gaps)**\n- The two tests that cite this requirement check the subject input only indirectly (position and presence). The complete-text assertions sit in uncited tests.\n- The resolution that yields the full fenced text lives in shared resolver code (`InputSelector::resolve`). That code is not marked with this requirement, but it is generic infrastructure for requirements selections.",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.authoring_input",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.authoring_input"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} authoring_input",
        "When authoring evidence for a requirement, its complete fenced declaration MUST",
        "be included in the mandatory input set defined by",
        "{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} mandatory_inputs",
        "The harness MUST construct each assessment's mandatory input set by combining",
        "the contributions specified by the requirements applicable to that subject and",
        "its inputs. A requirement that contributes inputs MUST cite this requirement and",
        "define which sources it contributes and when. The harness MUST include every",
        "applicable contribution, deduplicate identical selections, and report",
        "unresolvable mandatory inputs before inference. The complete set MUST be supplied",
        "in the initial model request.",
        "Generated output under project-root `evidence/`, including symlink aliases, MUST",
        "be omitted from mandatory inputs when the default exclusion applies. An explicit",
        "workspace allow MUST permit those selections; an explicit disallow MUST abort",
        "under {cite}`requirements.authoring.read_denial`.",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
